# N16 - Cross-run recall (bookshelf): DONE

Committed as `85964cc` ("N16: cross-run recall bookshelf (recall/recall_status tools)")
on branch `khamis1992/neo-n16`. 12 files changed, 1402 insertions, 5 deletions.

Scope: index finished run-graph nodes (text, image, webpage, video) from past runs
with embeddings + metadata, and expose a `recall` tool that returns nodes from
earlier runs and attaches a chosen hit as a new node linked back to its origin.

## What N16 delivered

- `src/graph/recall.ts` (new) — the derived cross-run shelf.
  - `RecallIndex`: one JSON row per finished, human-usable node in the existing
    `entities` table (`recall_node`), keyed by the deterministic node id, so it
    survives restarts with no new file or migration.
  - `recallText` / `recallMedium` / `isRecallable`: which nodes are shelf-worthy and
    the text that gets embedded. Media nodes carry no text of their own, so the
    description is built from the metadata that is there (title, path, query,
    summary), which is what makes "the dashboard image from the Nutrio report"
    findable later. `media` splits into `image`/`video` from the node's own
    `kind`/`mime`.
  - Hybrid search: cosine over the embedded vectors when an embedder is available,
    with a dependency-free BM25 fallback so a run still recalls its own past work
    when Ollama is down. A vector channel with no hit falls through to lexical, so
    a paraphrase the embedder missed is still found.
  - `rebuild(store)` re-reads the run graph from scratch and repairs anything the
    incremental sync missed; `scheduleSync` / `runSync` coalesce a burst of writes
    into one embedding pass.
  - Three properties by design: the index is derived so it can be rebuilt; it
    degrades instead of breaking; and it is throwaway-safe, so a recall failure can
    never break the task.
- `src/tools/memory.ts` — `createRecallTools()`: the `recall` tool (list candidates,
  or attach one by `node_id`) and `recall_status` (what the shelf holds, and whether
  vector search is available). Both answer an honest empty result instead of
  throwing.
- `src/graph/runGraph.ts` — `attachRecall()` gives the live graph a shelf,
  `attachRecallNode()` files a recalled hit into the current run as a `note` node
  whose payload names the origin node id, run id, type and the query that surfaced
  it, carrying over the origin's text/path/url when it still exists.
- `src/agent/runGraphAdapter.ts` — attaches the process-wide shelf to the live graph
  on `beginRun`, and a deleted run now also leaves the shelf.
- `src/tools/registry.ts` — new `ToolRegistry.withBound()`: replaces an
  already-present tool of the same name instead of colliding, for run-scoped
  instances of a process-wide tool.
- `src/index.ts` — rebuilds the recall tool instances per run with the run id and
  the callback that files the hit into that run, bound via `withBound`.
- `src/setup/runtime.ts` — registers the shelf and the tools process-wide.
- `src/server/runTools.ts` — `recall` and `recall_status` are always-on, because
  this is a read over the user's own past work plus one node write.
- `src/agent/insights.ts`, `src/agent/sideChat.ts` — `recall` counts as a memory
  tool and as a side-chat-mutating tool.

## Acceptance test (automated and passing)

"Asset made in run A is found by description in run B and attached as a node with a
link back" is covered by `src/graph/__tests__/recall.test.ts`: a node published by
`artifact_published` in run A is indexed, found by description through the `recall`
tool, and attached into run B as a node whose payload carries `recalled: true`, the
source node id, the source run id and the query.

## Bugs the tests caught (all real, all fixed)

1. **Zero-score hits passed the filter.** BM25 and vector scores of `0` satisfied
   `>= minScore` (default 0), so a filter-only query returned every node of that
   medium. `search()` now requires `score > 0`.
2. **The tool reported the wrong error.** `recall` checked attach-wiring before node
   existence, so a missing node was misreported as "attaching not wired". Reordered.
3. **Stale rows in `rebuild`.** A row could outlive the node it described; `rebuild`
   now compares identity *and* run provenance, not just the text.
4. **A run-scoped tool could not replace a process-wide one.** `withTools` calls
   `register`, which throws on a duplicate name, so the per-run recall instances
   would have crashed the run. Added `withBound` with a regression test asserting
   `withTools` throws on the duplicate while `withBound` succeeds.

## Gates (all run from the worktree root on committed HEAD 85964cc)

- `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`                -> exit 0
- `npm run build:server`                             -> exit 0 (server bundle + pdf worker written to dist/)
- `(cd packages/web && npx next build)`              -> exit 0 (Compiled successfully, TypeScript finished)
