## N21: Arabic/RTL pass for the visual canvas and the Knowledge Garden

### What this task delivered

Extended Arabic and RTL support across two surfaces, per `.neo-ref/AGENT_NEO_RE.md`
and `docs/BUILD_SPEC.md`. Committed as `af1b981` on `khamis1992/neo-n21`.

1. **Visual canvas (run graph).** `packages/web/src/components/workspace/canvas/layout.ts`
   gained `LayoutOptions.direction` and a `siblingX` helper that mirrors a layer's
   column index when the direction is `rtl`. A layer is still read start-to-end,
   so in RTL the first sibling sits at the right-hand edge; mirroring the index
   (rather than negating x) keeps every coordinate non-negative and the row inside
   the same span, so the scroll surface, bounds and pan/zoom transform need no RTL
   branch. Layers still stack top-to-bottom.
   `CanvasPanel.tsx` derives the direction from the panel locale
   (`isRtl = locale === 'ar'`) and lets an explicit `layoutOptions.direction`
   override win, which is also the test seam. Node labels get `dir="auto"` so
   Arabic text renders in its own direction inside an otherwise-LTR panel.

2. **Knowledge Garden (bilingual retrieval and splitting).**
   - `src/knowledge/text.ts` is the single shared normalizer. `normalizeText` folds
     Arabic spelling variants (ta marbuta to ha, alef maksura and Persian yaa to
     yaa, every hamza carrier to a bare alef or its base letter), drops diacritics
     and the tatweel, then lowercases. `tokenize`/`queryTerms` are script-aware and
     strip both languages' stop terms. `sentences`, `SPLIT_RUN` and `isHeadingLine`
     treat Arabic punctuation (`. ! ? ؟ ۔`) and deliberately never cut on the
     Arabic comma `،`, which separates a phrase rather than a sentence.
   - `src/knowledge/splitter.ts` is sentence-bounded by `SPLIT_RUN`: every seed
     closes on a sentence mark, a short paragraph is kept whole under the
     `MIN_CHUNK_CHARS` floor instead of being shredded, and a short Arabic sentence
     is not mistaken for a heading.
   - `src/neoEval/knowledge.ts` reuses the shared `normalizeText`/`queryTerms`, so a
     KB-grounded fixture written in Arabic retrieves exactly the way the shipped
     feature does.
   - `src/tools/attachments.ts` gained `tidyPdfText`, which strips pdf-parse page
     footers and separator rules, and folds the extracted Arabic PDF text with the
     same normalizer before it is stored or split. Reading order is pdfjs's own bidi
     pass (logical, not reversed), and the test pins the counter-case that states
     why no reversal is applied.

### Acceptance tests (automated, all passing)

| Test | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `src/knowledge/__tests__/arabic.test.ts` | 14 pass |
| `src/knowledge/__tests__/arabicPdf.test.ts` | 9 pass (real pdfjs worker, real Identity-H PDF) |
| `src/server/__tests__/knowledgeRoutes.test.ts` | 15 pass |
| `packages/web/.../canvas/__tests__/layout.test.ts` | pass |
| `packages/web/.../canvas/__tests__/CanvasPanel.test.ts` | 14 pass |
| `packages/web/.../settings/__tests__/knowledgeGardenPane.test.tsx` | 7 pass |

The Arabic PDF acceptance test builds a real one-page Identity-H PDF (ToUnicode
CMap, visual-order glyph runs) and drives the real importer, splitter and pdfjs
worker: the text extracts in logical order, imports as seeds that split on their
own sentence marks, and is stored folded so `الفوترة` and `الفاتورة` are one
retrievable term.

Playwright is not available in this harness, so the RTL screenshot review called
for by the task is covered by the vitest component tests instead and documented
here: `CanvasPanel.test.tsx` asserts the row mirrors from the right under `rtl`,
that an Arabic locale mirrors by default, that an explicit `ltr` override still
wins, and that a single-node layer is not mirrored.

