## N18 validation pass (2026-10-23): mutation testing found a coverage gap, now closed

A follow-up validation pass re-ran every claimed gate and, more importantly, tried to
*falsify* each claim by reverting the fix and re-running the test. That is what found the
problem below. Commit `9f76f9a` (1 file, +63/-1) on top of `9f28ce1`.

### The claim that was wrong

The earlier note said the new browser spec caught the dropped-placement bug. It did not.
With `types.ts` reverted to the pre-fix `coerceCanvasNode`, all 7 tests still passed.

### Root cause

The panel receives nodes from two sources, and only one of them coerces:

- the initial HTTP load, `CanvasClient.getGraph` -> `coerceCanvasGraphResponse`, which runs
  `coerceCanvasLayout` and therefore `coerceCanvasLayoutNode`;
- the SSE snapshot, `CanvasStream`, which hands `snapshot.nodes` straight to `setNodes` and
  never coerces.

The spec served both, so the snapshot always won the race and the coercion path was never
exercised. The test passed for the wrong reason: the coordinates came from the snapshot, not
from the code under test.

### The fix

- a `nodePosition` helper reads a card's `transform` back as numbers and fails on a
  non-finite transform. A dropped `x`/`y` renders `translate(NaNpx, NaNpx)`, which the browser
  discards as invalid, so the observable failure is an empty string;
- a `refuseStream` option 404s the SSE endpoint, forcing the panel down its own HTTP graph
  load, which is the only path that runs the graph coercion;
- a new test asserts the server's exact coordinates, `(40,40)` and
  `(40 + GRAPH_SPAN, 40 + GRAPH_SPAN)`, survive that path.

### Mutation table (each fix reverted individually against the 8-test spec)

| reverted | result |
|---|---|
| `types.ts` (placement) | 2 failed: `node n1 has a finite transform, got ` |
| `annotationsClient.ts` | 4 failed |
| `client.ts` | 1 failed |
| `stream.ts` | 1 failed |
| none (committed tree) | 8 passed, stable across 3 consecutive runs |

Every fix is therefore caught by a test that fails without it. The unit test
(`__tests__/types.test.ts`) also fails on the reverted `types.ts` (1 failed of 10), so the
placement fix is covered at both levels.

### Gates re-run on the committed tree (worktree root)

- `(cd packages/web && npx vitest run)`  -> Test Files 84 passed (84), Tests 588 passed (588).
- `(cd packages/web && npx tsc --noEmit)` -> exit 0.
- `npx tsc --noEmit`                      -> exit 0.
- `npm run build:server`                  -> exit 0.
- `(cd packages/web && npx next build)`   -> exit 0.
- `node --import tsx --test src/graph/__tests__/search.test.ts src/server/__tests__/canvasNotes.test.ts src/server/__tests__/graphRoutes.test.ts`
                                           -> tests 38, pass 38, fail 0.
- `(cd packages/web && npx playwright test specs/canvas-n18.spec.ts)` -> 8 passed (3 runs).
- `(cd packages/web && npx playwright test specs/login.spec.ts specs/task.spec.ts)` -> 3 passed.
- `npm run check:encoding`                -> OK (no BOM, no mojibake, all JSON valid).

Also verified: the committed file list touches no forbidden path, no secret appears in any new
file, and the spec was normalised to CRLF to match `core.autocrlf` after the edit tool
rewrote it as LF.

